\f0\b\fs48 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Session Composition & Program Settings Taxonomy for Helm: Engineering Specification & Scientific Framework\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf2 1. Executive Summary\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 The current architecture of Helm evaluates daily volume allocation through a simplistic 1:1 muscle-to-exercise assignment model. In 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 PrescriptionEngine
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 , a session is constructed by assigning exactly one exercise to each target muscle defined within the active day's split. On a Pull day\'97defined in 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 SessionSplitPlanner
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  as back and biceps\'97the engine selects one movement for the back and one movement for the biceps. When the weekly volume target for back (e.g., 12 hard sets) is distributed across two weekly Pull exposures, the engine assigns 6 hard sets to the single back exercise in a single workout.\
This 1:1 design produces a severe structural failure: thin sessions consisting of only 2 exercises, where total weekly set targets are met by dumping high set volumes onto single movements.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb6 \strokec5 Current Helm Engine Allocation (Pull Day Example):\
  Back Muscle Target  ---> 1 Exercise Picked (e.g., Lat Pulldown) ---> Assigned 6 Hard Sets\
  Bicep Muscle Target ---> 1 Exercise Picked (e.g., Bicep Curl)   ---> Assigned 5 Hard Sets\
  Result: 2 Exercises, 11 Sets (Violates Gym Practice Norms & Biomechanical Principles)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 From a sports science and neuromuscular perspective, dumping 5 to 6 hard sets onto a single exercise in one session introduces significant physiological drawbacks:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Intra-Session Diminishing Returns and Junk Volume
\f1\b0 : Empirical meta-regressions demonstrate that hypertrophic stimulus per set decays rapidly past 3 to 4 sets for a given movement vector in a single session. Beyond this threshold, acute localized neuromuscular fatigue, calcium ion handling impairment, and excitation-contraction coupling degradation accumulate, yielding "junk volume" that elevates systemic fatigue without stimulating additional muscle protein synthesis.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Inadequate Regional Muscle Activation
\f1\b0 : Complex muscle groups like the latissimus dorsi, trapezius, and quadriceps cannot be fully stimulated by a single biomechanical movement. Biomechanical research confirms that heterogeneous muscle growth (regional hypertrophy) requires varied loading angles, resistance profiles, and muscle lengths. A single vertical pull fails to recruit the mid-trapezius and rhomboids as effectively as a horizontal row.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Product Defect
\f1\b0 : A two-exercise session fails to align with realistic gym training durations, rendering the application's prescriptions incomplete to lifters regardless of mathematical accuracy.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 To resolve this defect, Helm must adopt a 
\f0\b Movement Pattern Session Composer Architecture
\f1\b0 . This framework shifts the unit of session design from gross muscle labels to functional movement pattern slots (e.g., Vertical Pull, Horizontal Pull, Elbow Flexion). The composer enforces a maximum cap of 3 to 4 sets per exercise, dynamically expands pattern slots based on the athlete's explicit Session Duration Budget, and splits muscle volume across complimentary movement vectors.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf2 2. Program Settings Information Architecture & Taxonomy\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 To prevent user interface settings from corrupting the deterministic math engine, Helm requires a clear taxonomy that classifies settings by their operational scope: top-level engine parameters, runtime filters, or non-calculating educational resources.\

